WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:06.920
BABATUNDE OGUNLADE:
Hello, everyone.

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:09.003
Today we're going to be
working through Goodie Bag

00:00:09.003 --> 00:00:11.300
5, which is on electronic
materials, namely

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:12.380
semiconductors.

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:14.030
In order to follow
along, you'll need

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:16.520
a multimeter, a pair
of alligator clips,

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:17.670
some large LEDs--

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:20.240
I'll be using red,
white, blue, and green--

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:21.680
and some regular sized LEDs.

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:23.972
I'll be using red,
blue, and purple.

00:00:23.972 --> 00:00:26.180
Our main objectives today
are to identify differently

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:28.580
colored LEDs by
irradiating them with light

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:30.510
across the visible
light spectrum.

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:32.630
And in the process, to
explore the relationship

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:34.610
between the bandgap
of a semiconductor

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:36.433
and its critical
absorption wavelength.

00:00:36.433 --> 00:00:37.850
As we work through
the Goodie Bag,

00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:40.190
I'd like you to think about
how the size of the bandgap

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:42.920
of material may influence
which wavelengths of light

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:44.120
you can absorb.

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:45.690
Let's get started.

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:47.690
First, connect your
black or negative lead

00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:50.000
to the 10 amp port
on the multimeter,

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:51.950
and then your red
or positive lead

00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:54.590
to the voltage
ohm milliamp port.

00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:57.410
Then connect the probe end
of each respective lead

00:00:57.410 --> 00:00:59.810
to one end of an alligator clip.

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:01.340
After you do this,
you should still

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:03.860
have two free ends of
the alligator clips.

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:07.100
Grab one of your small LEDs
and connect the free end

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:10.160
of the alligator clip
connected to the red lead probe

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:12.080
to the positive end of the LED.

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:14.150
This is the longer
leg of the LED.

00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:15.860
And then connect
the other free end

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:17.810
that's connected to
the black lead probe

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:19.490
to the negative end of the LED.

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:21.410
That's the short leg of the LED.

00:01:21.410 --> 00:01:24.110
Then turn on your
multimeter and set the dial

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:26.790
to 2,000 milli DC volts.

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:29.480
This is the order of
magnitude of voltage

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:31.430
we'll measure across
our small LEDs

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:34.220
when we irradiate them with
light from the larger LEDs.

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:36.480
Finally, make sure to
keep track of which small

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:39.020
LED you're testing
so that which LED

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:40.850
to refer to when
we start comparing

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:43.520
data between small LEDs.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:46.940
So now we have our small LED
hooked up to our multimeter.

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:49.760
And now we're going to shine
each one of our large LEDs

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:52.190
onto the small LED and
record whether or not we have

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:54.240
some non-zero voltage reading.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:56.450
If the large LED
has enough energy

00:01:56.450 --> 00:02:00.440
to excite electrons across
the bandgap of the small LED,

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:03.840
we should see some non-zero
voltage across the small LED.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:06.860
So first I'm going to try
and shine this blue light

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:09.607
onto my small LED, and I'm
going to look at the multimeter

00:02:09.607 --> 00:02:11.440
and try to see if there's
some voltage drop.

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:18.980
As you can see, our
volt meter is now

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:21.050
measuring some
non-zero value, which

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:23.810
means that this blue light
has enough energy to excite

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:26.540
electrons across its bandgap.

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:30.350
After you've done this, repeat
this for the other large LEDs,

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:32.780
and then repeat the entire
experiment for the other two

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:33.860
small LEDs.

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:36.620
This should generate a
set of 12 data points.

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:38.870
And using our knowledge
of bandgaps and wavelength

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:41.540
absorption, as well as the
data that you've acquired,

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:45.070
we're going to determine
which small LED is what color.

00:02:45.070 --> 00:02:46.820
So after running through
your experiments,

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:49.160
you should have some chart
that looks like mine, where

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:51.880
I have my small LEDs
going down the side here

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:53.930
and the large LEDs going across.

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:56.290
So each square
represents a time when

00:02:56.290 --> 00:02:59.240
a small LED he was
irradiated by a large LED.

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:02.810
And a check represents when a
non-zero voltage was measured

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:05.390
across the multimeter,
and an x represents

00:03:05.390 --> 00:03:07.820
when there was a
zero or very low

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:11.060
voltage reading across the LED.

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:14.300
So in order to dig
into what this means

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:16.460
it's important to
understand the balance

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:19.830
of the visible light spectrum in
terms of energy and wavelength.

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:21.800
So if you remember the
visible light spectrum,

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:27.580
known wonderfully by the
acronym ROYGBIV, where

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:31.340
you have red on one end and you
have violet on the other end,

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:35.440
we know that red
corresponds to around 700

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:37.600
nanometers in wavelength.

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:40.000
And we know that violet
or purple corresponds

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:44.080
to around 40 nanometers
of wavelength.

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:46.660
And if we think back to the
relationship between the energy

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:49.720
of light and its wavelength,
we know that the formula

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:53.020
is E equals hc over lambda.

00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:58.270
So as the wavelength of light
decreases, the energy of light

00:03:58.270 --> 00:03:59.560
increases.

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:03.940
So going across, as we go from
red, green, blue, and white,

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:05.740
we have increasing
energy because we

00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:07.530
have decreasing wavelength.

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:10.540
And just as a reminder,
white is a composite

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:12.140
of red, blue, and green.

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:15.120
So it has all three of
these combined in it.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:19.240
So now let's start to identify
which LED is what color.

00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:21.170
So if you look at
small LED 1, it

00:04:21.170 --> 00:04:24.720
was irradiated by red,
green, blue, and white light.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:28.920
And there was a voltage drop
across the LED for all of them.

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:30.510
That means that
at the very least,

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:33.840
red had enough energy
to excite electrons

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:36.630
across the bandgap
of that small LED.

00:04:36.630 --> 00:04:38.363
Because that's the
case, and we know

00:04:38.363 --> 00:04:39.780
that we're dealing
with LEDs which

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:41.800
emit like in the
visible light spectrum,

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:47.280
we know that this small
LED then must be red.

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:50.410
So if we look at
small LED 2, we can

00:04:50.410 --> 00:04:52.360
see that both red and
green light did not

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:54.460
have enough energy
to excite electrons

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:56.410
across the bandgap
of the second LED.

00:04:56.410 --> 00:04:59.950
But when we shined blue
light onto the LED,

00:04:59.950 --> 00:05:01.780
we got a non-zero voltage drop.

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:03.970
And the same thing when we
shined white light, which

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:05.650
also has blue light in it.

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:07.820
Because, like I said
at the beginning,

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:11.140
we knew that our small these
were red, blue, and purple,

00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:13.330
and we've already
found our red LED,

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:19.150
that means that this
LED must be the blue.

00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:21.410
So finally, if we look
at small LED number 3,

00:05:21.410 --> 00:05:24.500
we can see that red, green,
blue, and white light

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:27.530
did not have sufficient energy
to cause some voltage drop

00:05:27.530 --> 00:05:28.940
across the LED.

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:30.920
If we look back
to ROYGBIV, and we

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:35.160
think about the
energy of red to blue,

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:37.100
that means that they
didn't have enough energy

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:39.420
to excite anything after it.

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:43.636
And that means that our final
LED must be violent or purple.

00:05:49.850 --> 00:05:52.580
So by shining light across
the visible light spectrum

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:55.280
onto our small LEDs,
we're able to identify

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:57.800
which one of our small
LEDs are what color.

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:01.520
So right here I have small LED
1 hooked up to the multimeter.

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:03.470
And we determined it was red.

00:06:03.470 --> 00:06:07.860
And so we can check that
by just turning on the LED.

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:09.890
So as you can see,
this LED is shining

00:06:09.890 --> 00:06:11.650
red, which means
it's the red LED,

00:06:11.650 --> 00:06:14.150
and we're able to confirm that
by using our data as well.

00:06:14.150 --> 00:06:15.770
You can check that
the other two LEDs

00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:17.240
are the colors
that we determined

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:19.320
by doing the same thing.

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:22.130
So today we looked at a direct
application of semiconductors

00:06:22.130 --> 00:06:23.540
in LEDs.

00:06:23.540 --> 00:06:26.880
We looked at the relationship
between the bandgap of an LED,

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:29.450
of a semiconductor, and
the range of light that

00:06:29.450 --> 00:06:31.910
can absorb across its bandgap.

00:06:31.910 --> 00:06:34.440
We found that for
semiconductors, there

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:36.190
are some critical
absorption wavelengths,

00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:40.550
some maximum wavelength, below
which the LED will absorb

00:06:40.550 --> 00:06:43.090
all other wavelengths of light.

